+Hard-coded buffer offsets break on every game patch. The fix is to find what you
+need at runtime and *validate* it, as described in the roadmap: locate a
+view-projection matrix by its behaviour — does it project a known point into the
+clip volume, does it change smoothly between frames, does the camera position it
+implies track the player? — rather than by where it was last month.
+
+The architectural payoff is already in place: when detection fails, the
+capability graph reports `BLOCKED` with a reason and the features decline to run.
+It does not crash, and it does not run on a matrix it guessed wrong.
+
+The same applies to the pinned provider build. The live-control adapter refuses
+anything that is not the exact verified binary — size, hash, code fingerprints and
+dispatch identity must all match — and falls back to restart-based configuration
+rather than writing to offsets it cannot vouch for.
+
+---
+
+## 8. Diagnosability as a feature
+
+When a closed, authenticated mod ghosts, its user can do nothing but report it.
+They cannot see which input failed, and neither can anyone helping them.
+
+This project already has the machinery to be different: every capability carries
+evidence with a source and a trust level, `ACTIVE` decays when it stops being
+re-proven, and the status page names the root cause once rather than printing a
+cascade.
+
+Extend it in one direction: a **debug visualisation** that renders what the
+generator is actually being handed — the motion buffer, the confidence field, the
+HUD-less capture, and what has been classified as interface. Most ghosting
+complaints resolve in seconds when you can see that the motion buffer is black
+where a vehicle is.
+
+That is a real advantage over any closed solution, and it costs a day of work.
+
+---
+
+## 9. What "ghosting-free" honestly means
+
+It does not mean artifact-free, and claiming otherwise would repeat exactly the
+mistake this project exists to avoid.
+
+**Removable by correct engineering:**
+
+- smearing on vehicles and pedestrians → per-object motion (Stage 5)
+- interface smearing and warping → capture and composite separately (Stage 3)
+- ghosting during menus and loading → suspend generation (already implemented)
+- trails from wrong motion → validation and fallback (section 2)
+
+**Fundamental, and manageable rather than removable:**
+
+- **Disocclusion.** What was hidden last frame has no history. DLSS handles it,
+  imperfectly, by construction.
+- **Transparency.** Glass, particles, rain and alpha-tested foliage have no
+  single depth or motion. This is what the reactive mask exists for; it reduces
+  the artifact, it does not delete it.
+- **Very fast motion.** Beyond some velocity there is not enough correlation
+  between frames to reconstruct from.
+
+An honest claim is: *no structural ghosting — every artifact that remains is one
+the underlying technique cannot remove, and the interface can show you which one
+you are looking at.*
+
+That is a stronger position than "ghosting-free", because it is true, and because
+it is checkable.
+
+---
+
+## 10. Order of attack
+
+The sequencing in `ROADMAP.md` still holds, with the additions from this document
+folded in:
+
+| Stage | Adds | Why this order |
+|---|---|---|
+| 1 | camera constants, detected at runtime | everything depends on it |
+| 2 | reprojected motion — **the base layer** | image pass improves immediately; failing-closed foundation |
+| 2b | motion validation + confidence field | makes every later stage debuggable |
+| 3 | HUD-less, UI layer, **NUI separation** | highest value per hour on an RP server |
+| 4 | D3D11↔D3D12 interop, DLSS-G | generator runs at all |
+| 5 | per-object motion for movers only | removes structural ghosting; unlocks NR+FG together |
+| 6 | upscaling, with the game's TAA disabled | most invasive, least certain payoff |
+
+The reordering that matters: **stage 2 before anything about the generator.**
+Not because it is easier, but because it changes what a failure costs for every
+stage after it. Building the fallback first is what lets stage 5 be a quality
+improvement instead of a correctness requirement.
+
+---
+
+## 11. The question that decides whether any of this is worth it
+
+Unchanged from the roadmap, and it is not a technical question:
+
+> On the servers I actually play, is a full add-on ReShade stack with patched
+> shaders permitted?
+
+Stages 2 through 6 are exactly the class of client modification that Pure Mode
+and server anticheat exist to block. A better architecture does not change that,
+and neither will a better implementation.
+
+Measure before committing, too. GTA V on a populated RP server is frequently
+CPU-bound, and upscaling helps only when you are GPU-bound. Stage 6 is months of
+the most invasive work in this document; find out first whether it would move
+your frame rate at all.
diff --git a/v3/docs/ROADMAP.md b/v3/docs/ROADMAP.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..3f3bdcc
--- /dev/null
+++ b/v3/docs/ROADMAP.md
@@ -0,0 +1,638 @@
+# SECRET EMKO — Roadmap to Upscaling, Frame Generation and Neural Rendering together
+
+## The short version
+
+All three features want the same thing, and GTA V Legacy does not produce it:
+
+```
+                    ┌─────────────────────────────┐
+                    │  geometry motion vectors    │
+                    │  + depth                    │
+                    │  + camera constants         │
+                    │  + HUD-less colour / UI     │
+                    └──────────────┬──────────────┘
+                                   │
+              ┌────────────────────┼────────────────────┐
+              ▼                    ▼                    ▼
+        Neural Rendering    Frame Generation      Upscaling
+```
+
+There is no shortcut where one of them arrives early on its own. Build the
+inputs, and all three become reachable. That is why this document is a single
+chain rather than three parallel efforts.
+
+Two constraints shape everything below.
+
+**GTA V does not output motion vectors.** The engine never had a use for them.
+They have to be either reconstructed from depth (exact for the camera, wrong for
+anything that moves on its own) or injected into the game's own shaders (correct,
+and most of the work in this document).
+
+**DLSS Frame Generation does not run on D3D11.** Streamline requires D3D12 or
+Vulkan for DLSS-G; GTA V Legacy is D3D11. The way this is solved in practice is a
+D3D11↔D3D12 interop layer: a second D3D12 device, resources shared through NT
+handles, synchronisation through shared fences, and the generator running on the
+D3D12 side. This is a substantial piece of engineering in its own right and it
+sits on the critical path for frame generation only.
+
+---
+
+## Stage 0 — where the code is now
+
+Shipped in v3:
+
+- shader classification that accumulates across sessions (`SecretEMKO-FG.candidates.txt`)
+- a base-frame limiter that owns pacing
+- the capability graph, arbitration and fail-closed status that the stages below
+  plug into without rework
+
+The capability nodes `ShaderMotion`, `DepthInput`, `HudlessColor`, `UiLayer`,
+`FrameConstants` and `PresentControl` already exist, already gate the interface,
+and are already wired to the provider's status flags. Each stage below lights one
+or more of them up; nothing in `core/` has to change to accommodate that.
+
+---
+
+## Stage 1 — Camera constants
+
+**Goal.** Know the view and projection matrices for the current and previous
+frame, aligned to the presented frame.
+
+**How.** GTA uploads its per-frame constants through buffer maps. In the ReShade
+add-on API the practical hooks are `map_buffer_region` / `unmap_buffer_region`
+plus the descriptor binding events; you watch constant-buffer-sized writes and
+look for a 4×4 float matrix that behaves like a view-projection matrix.
+
+Identifying it at runtime rather than hard-coding an offset is worth the effort —
+offsets move with every game patch. A detector that works:
+
+1. treat every 64-byte-aligned float block in the buffer as a candidate
+2. project a point that must be on screen (the origin of camera space) and check
+   `w > 0` and `x/w, y/w` inside the clip volume
+3. require temporal continuity — a real view-projection matrix changes smoothly
+   between frames, a coincidental float pattern does not
+4. cross-check by reconstructing the camera position and confirming it moves when
+   the player moves
+
+**Ships.** Nothing visible. `FrameConstants` reaches `READY`.
+
+**Verification.** Log the reconstructed camera position and compare it against
+in-game movement. If it tracks, the matrix is right.
+
+**Effort.** Days, not weeks. This is the cheapest stage and everything else
+depends on it.
+
+---
+
+## Stage 2 — Depth, and motion reconstructed from it
+
+**Goal.** A dense motion vector buffer that is exactly correct for camera
+movement.
+
+**How.** Find the scene depth buffer (ReShade's own depth detection heuristics
+apply: the depth-stencil view with the most draws at backbuffer aspect ratio).
+Determine the depth convention empirically — sample near and far geometry and see
+which end is which, rather than assuming; GTA's convention should be confirmed on
+the actual build you target, not taken from documentation.
+
+Then, in a compute shader, per pixel:
+
+```
+depth  →  unproject with inverse(viewProj_now)   →  world position
+world  →  project with viewProj_previous          →  previous screen position
+motion =  previous_uv − current_uv
+```
+
+Write to an `R16G16_FLOAT` target.
+
+**What this gets right and wrong.** It is *exact* for everything static, which in
+a driving game is most of the screen most of the time. It is *wrong* for anything
+that moves under its own power — vehicles, pedestrians, animated props — because
+reprojection treats them as part of the static world. Those objects will ghost.
+
+That trade is acceptable here for one reason: it is the foundation Stage 5 builds
+on, and it is the first time the neural pass runs on real motion rather than
+estimated motion.
+
+**A hybrid worth considering.** Reprojected motion and the existing optical-flow
+path fail in opposite places. Reprojection is exact for the camera and blind to
+independent movement; optical flow sees independent movement but guesses
+everything. Using reprojection as the base and optical flow only as a residual
+where the two disagree is a real option, and it is cheaper than Stage 5.
+
+**Required code change.** The provider currently publishes one
+`kProviderGeometryMotion` flag. Split it:
+
+```cpp
+kProviderCameraMotion    // reprojected from depth — exact for the camera
+kProviderGeometryMotion  // per-object — correct for moving objects too
+```
+
+Arbitration must keep treating camera-only motion as **insufficient for frame
+generation**. Neural rendering may use it; the generator may not. That preserves
+the fail-closed promise rather than quietly lowering the bar.
+
+**Ships.** Neural rendering on real camera motion. `DepthInput` reaches `READY`,
+`CameraMotion` reaches `READY`.
+
+**Effort.** Weeks.
+
+---
+
+## Stage 3 — HUD-less colour and the interface layer
+
+**Goal.** The scene image before the interface is drawn, and the interface on its
+own.
+
+**How.** Find the end-of-scene point: the last draw with depth bound before the
+run of interface draws. The candidate file the provider has been accumulating
+since Stage 0 is exactly the data for this — interface draws cluster as
+no-depth, alpha-blended, with orthographic projection in their constant buffer.
+
+Copy the render target at that point into a persistent texture. For the interface
+layer, either capture it into a cleared target of its own, or reconstruct alpha by
+differencing the final image against the HUD-less copy.
+
+**Ships.** Interface protection that actually does something. `HudlessColor` and
+`UiLayer` reach `READY`. Also immediately useful to neural rendering: the pass
+stops touching the map, chat and menus.
+
+**Effort.** Weeks. Mostly experimentation — this is the stage where the
+accumulated classification pays for itself.
+
+---
+
+## Stage 4 — D3D11 ↔ D3D12 interop, then DLSS-G
+
+**Goal.** Run the generator at all.
+
+**How.**
+
+1. Create a D3D12 device on the same adapter.
+2. Create the textures that have to cross the boundary as D3D11 resources with
+   `D3D11_RESOURCE_MISC_SHARED_NTHANDLE`, get a handle through
+   `IDXGIResource1::CreateSharedHandle`, open it on the D3D12 side.
+3. Synchronise with a shared fence (`ID3D11Device5::CreateFence` with the shared
+   flag, opened on D3D12). Do not use queries or flush-and-pray; the failure mode
+   is a torn frame that is very hard to attribute.
+4. Tag depth, motion, HUD-less colour and UI through Streamline, set the camera
+   constants, evaluate DLSS-G on the D3D12 queue.
+5. Present from whichever side owns the swap chain, and handle device loss on
+   both sides without freezing the presented image.
+
+**The risk.** This is the stage most likely to produce crashes that are hard to
+diagnose, because a synchronisation bug shows up as corruption several frames
+later. Budget time for building a deterministic repro harness before building the
+feature.
+
+**Ships.** Frame generation 2x/3x — with visible ghosting on vehicles and
+pedestrians, because Stage 5 has not happened yet. Whether to ship that state to
+users or keep it behind the expert unlock is a judgement call; the honest answer
+is to keep it locked until Stage 5, which is what the current arbitration already
+does.
+
+**Effort.** Weeks to months.
+
+---
+
+## Stage 5 — Per-object motion vectors
+
